  <content>
  <section><title>Graphing Linear Inequalities</title>
<para/>
<para>We can also represent inequalities using interval notation. There is no upper end to the solution to this inequality. In interval notation, we express \( x &gt; 3 \) as \((3, ∞)\). The symbol ∞ is read as “infinity.” It is not an actual number.</para>
<para>The figure below shows both the number line and the interval notation.</para>
<para>                                                                        \(x &gt; 3\)<figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 3. The number line to the right of 3 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f11f9f52563f8687b96d97d1f8d166c69c5b4d7"/></media></figure></para>
<para>The inequality \(x≤1\) means all numbers less than or equal to 1. Here we need to show that 1 is a solution, too. We do that by putting a closed circle at \(x = 1\). We then shade in all the numbers to the left of 1, to show that all numbers less than 1 are solutions. See the figure below.</para>
<para>                                                   \( x ≤ 1 \)<figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 1. The number line to the left of 1 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c89f24114c86a88a457ff1ee55c0119e4aea0a91"/></media></figure></para>
<para id="docs-internal-guid-17ab7c97-7fff-0e49-3be6-d15506d843a8">There is no lower end to those numbers. We write \(x ≤ 1 \) in interval notation as \( (−∞,1] \). The symbol −\( ∞ \) is read as “negative infinity.” The figure below shows both the number line and interval notation.</para>
<title>Inequalities, Number Lines, and Interval Notation</title>
<figure><media alt="Four different number lines are shown.The &gt; symbol corresponds to an open circle and the number line to the right being highlighted. Its set notation is (a, ∞). The &lt; symbol corresponds to a closed circle and the number line to the right being highlighted. Its set notation is [a, ∞). The ≥ symbol corresponds to a closed circle and the number line to the right being highlighted. Its set notation is (–∞, a). The ≤ symbol corresponds to a closed circle and the number line to the left being highlighted. Its set notation is (–∞, a]."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ae108513af5043e10799df8d1eed5b40269dbece"/></media></figure><title>Try It: Graphing Linear Inequalities</title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2 use the following inequality:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>\( x &gt; -3 \)</para>
</blockquote>
<!-- BEGIN CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<!--Q#1-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the inequality on a number line.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on &#10;–3. The number line to the right of –3 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/10effd40cd28ca12937e70f7415e5166edad0307"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<!--Q #2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the inequality in interval notation.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \((-3, ∞)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<para>For questions 3 and 4 use the following inequality:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>\( x ≤ 5 \)</para>
</blockquote>
<!-- BEGIN CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<!--Q#3-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the inequality on a number line.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt=" A number line is shown. There is a closed circle &#10;on 5. The number line to the left of 5 is highlighted.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b549d66de4f02bdbf801752a89ee06481e8d9689"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END CLICK TO REVEAL NO TEXTBOX -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<!--Q #2-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the inequality in interval notation.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \((∞, 5]\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
